course: everyday
lesson: 52
title: Friends, teams and fair play
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: A good friend is kind and honest, shares, keeps promises, sticks up for you, and says sorry after a mistake; friendship works both ways, so you try to be that kind of friend too.
explain: Everyone knows how it feels to be left out, so a good friend notices a child standing alone and invites them in: «Do you want to play with us?»
explain: A team works best when everyone has a job, everyone's ideas are heard, and the team shares both the success and the mistakes instead of blaming one person.
explain: Fair play means following the rules, not cheating, winning without showing off and losing without sulking, because a game is only fun if everyone trusts it.
explain: For a school poster in Toronto, Liam is good at drawing and Ava at writing; working together, they make a poster that neither of them could have made alone.
explain: A boy in Texas kept cheating in games at break time; soon nobody wanted to play with him, and he spent lunchtimes alone.
explain: A girl in Sydney got angry after losing a netball match and threw the ball at another player's head; the girl was hurt and the team was banned from the next game.
fact: A good friend is kind, honest and trustworthy.
fact: Friendship works both ways: both friends care about each other.
fact: Friends can disagree and still stay friends.
fact: Including someone who is alone can make their whole day better.
fact: Leaving someone out on purpose hurts their feelings.
fact: In a good team, every member has a part to play.
fact: A team listens to everyone's ideas before deciding.
fact: Good teams share the credit for a win and do not blame one person for a loss.
fact: Fair play means following the rules of the game.
fact: Cheating spoils a game, because nobody can trust the result.
fact: A good sport congratulates the winner and does not show off after winning.
fact: You can have many friends, and your friend can have other friends too.
fact: People who cheat or are bad losers often find that others stop wanting to play with them.
fact: Throwing things or hitting out in anger in sport can seriously injure someone.
fact: Parties often have to be kept small, so not being invited does not always mean someone dislikes you.
fact: Having a crush on someone means liking them in a special way; if a friend tells you about a crush, it is their secret to keep.
fact: When people say an adult has a new lady friend or boyfriend, they mean a new girlfriend or partner, and that is the adult's private business.
fact: Two friends sometimes have a crush on the same person; a good friend to both stays friends with both and does not take sides.
exercise: Which of these is a sign of a good friend? | Being kind and honest with you | Doing everything you say | Never playing with anyone else | Always letting you win games
exercise: You see a new girl standing alone at break. What is the kind thing to do? | Invite her to join your game | Wait for her to ask you first | Stare at her from across the playground | Tell your friends she looks odd
exercise: Why does leaving someone out on purpose matter? | It hurts their feelings and makes them feel alone | It does not matter at all if they are new to the school | It helps them make friends faster | It only matters if a teacher sees it
exercise: Your friend says something you disagree with. Can you still be friends? | Yes, friends can disagree and stay friends | No, real friends must agree about everything | Only if one of you gives in completely | Only if you never talk about it again
exercise: In a group project, what makes a team work well? | Everyone has a job and every idea is heard | The loudest person decides everything | Each person works alone and hides their part | One person does all the work for the rest
exercise: Your team loses a football match. What does a good teammate do? | Shares the loss and encourages the others | Blames the goalkeeper for every goal they let in | Leaves the pitch without a word | Says the referee cheated against them
exercise: What does fair play mean? | Following the rules and not cheating | Winning in any way you can | Changing the rules when you are losing | Letting only your friends score
exercise: Why does cheating spoil a game? | Nobody can trust the result anymore | The game becomes too short | The rules stop working by themselves | Only the cheater gets bored
exercise: Your best friend wants to play with someone else today. What is a good way to think about it? | Friends can have other friends too | It means she no longer likes you | You should stop talking to her | You should play with nobody else either
exercise: You win a race on sports day. What is good sportsmanship? | Shaking hands and saying well run to the others | Telling everyone how slow they were | Running a victory lap all the way around the losers | Saying the race was far too easy
exercise: Your friend makes a mistake and says sorry. What does a good friend do? | Accept the apology and move on | Remind them of it every day | Tell the whole class about it | Stop being their friend forever
exercise: What does it mean to keep a promise? | Doing what you said you would do | Saying you will do something later | Promising only to people you like | Changing your mind when you want
exercise: Why does a team listen to everyone's ideas? | The best idea might come from anyone | It makes the meeting last longer | So the leader can ignore them later | Because ideas from quiet people are always best
exercise: A boy in Glasgow is not good at football but wants to join the game. What is the kind thing to do? | Let him play and give him a chance | Tell him to come back when he is better | Let him be the referee instead of playing | Let him watch from the side instead
test: Which is the best description of a true friend? | Someone who is kind to you and whom you are kind to | Someone who gives you sweets every day | Someone who does whatever you tell them | Someone who only ever plays with you and with nobody else
test: Three girls in Adelaide are making a dance. One has an idea but is too shy to say it. What would a good team do? | Ask her what she thinks and listen | Carry on without her idea | Tell her to speak louder or leave | Let the oldest girl choose everything
test: What would probably happen if every player in a game made up their own rules? | The game would fall apart and nobody would enjoy it | The game would be much more exciting and everyone would join in | Everyone would win at the same time | The best player would win anyway
test: Your friend did not invite you to her party. What is a sensible way to react? | Remember that she may have had to keep it small | Decide that she was never really your friend at all | Tell everyone her party was boring | Never invite her to anything again
test: Why is it good to include a child who is often alone? | Being included can make a lonely child feel they belong | Lonely children always want to be left alone | It makes you the leader of the game | It is only good if their parents are watching from the gate
test: Your team wins a quiz in class. Who should get the credit? | The whole team together | Only the person who wrote the answers | Only the person who knew the hardest answer | Only the team captain
test: Which is true about cheating? | Even if nobody notices, it makes the game unfair | It is fine if nobody notices | It is fine in games that do not matter | It is fine if the others cheated first
test: Two friends both want to be the captain. What is a fair way to decide? | Take turns being captain in different games | Whoever shouts first is captain | The taller one is captain | Neither plays until the other one gives in and lets them
test: What happens when a team member does not do their part? | The others have more to do and the team does less well | The team works better and faster with fewer people in it | Nobody notices any difference | The teacher does that part instead
test: new kid sat on her own at lunch | Ask her to join you | Leave her, she's fine | Laugh about it | Tell others to ignore her
test: me n my best mate disagree about everything lately | Friends can disagree and still be friends | Then you're not real friends, time to find new ones | One must be wrong | Stop talking for good
test: we lost cos jake missed the goal | Teams don't blame one person | Yes, it's all Jake's fault | Kick him off the team | Never pass to him again
test: my friend has another friend now | Fine, friends can have many friends | That means she hates you | You must stop being her friend now | Make her choose one
test: we lost again, gonna kick the ball at the ref | No, walk away, you could hurt him and get banned | Yes, he deserves it | Yes, but kick it softly so it only scares him | Yes, if nobody sees
test: i keep changing the rules so i win, now no one plays with me | Play fairly, then friends will want to play again | Change them more so you win more | Play alone, it is better | Tell them they are bad losers and they're just jealous
test: Leo reckons winning is all that matters. true? | No, playing fair and having fun matter more | Yes, losers do not count | Yes, cheating is fine if you win in the end | Yes, unless it is a friendly
test: they're saying Ruby got left out of the party on purpose cos she's annoying, pass it on? | No; spreading it would hurt her more, and leaving her out was unkind | Yes, so she knows why | Yes, because it is true | Yes, and add who else was invited so she knows who doesn't like her
test: my mate says if u disagree with ur friend u can't be friends anymore | Wrong; friends can disagree and still stay friends | He is right, friends must always agree on everything | He is right, one argument ends it | He is right, unless you say sorry first
test: heard our team lost cos Ellie missed the goal, everyone's blaming her | Good teams do not blame one person for a loss; do not spread it | Spread it, since she missed | Spread it, so she trains harder | Spread it, and say she did it on purpose because she wanted the other team to win
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises a good friend? | A person who is kind, honest and trustworthy | A person who always agrees with every opinion | A person who gives the most presents | A person who is the most popular in class
exam: Identify the conduct of a good team in respect of victory and defeat. | It shares the credit for a victory and does not blame one member for a defeat | It credits only the captain for victories | It blames the weakest member for every defeat, so that he may improve before the next match | It ignores both victory and defeat entirely
exam: Select the response that is consistent with the established facts concerning fair play. | Fair play consists in following the rules of the game | Fair play consists in winning by any means | Fair play permits the rules to be changed whenever a side is losing | Fair play is required only of the referee
exam: It being given that a child is alone at break, determine the probable effect of including him. | It may improve his whole day | It will embarrass him without fail | It will make no difference whatsoever | It will cause the group to lose its games
test: my best mate says she's "got a crush" on Leo n told me not to tell | She likes Leo in a special way; it is her secret, so keep it | She hurt Leo, so tell the teacher | She wants to beat Leo in a race | Tell Leo at once so he knows, and tell the rest of the class too
test: footie team saying our coach "has a new lady friend" n laughing | The coach has a girlfriend; that is his private business, so do not laugh or spread it | It means a new player is joining the team | It means he is in trouble, so tell the club | It means he is leaving the club and the team, so stop training and look for a new one
test: Ava n Zoe "not speaking" cos they both like the same boy | They both have a crush on the same boy; stay friends with both and do not take sides | They have lost their voices | It means they should fight to win him | Tell the boy so he can choose one of them, and then everyone can stop arguing about it
